              <p class="text-[#16A34A] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Siding</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">Washing Vinyl Siding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">The angle rule that keeps water out of the wall.</p>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Some surfaces have no safe setting. Roof shingles lose the granules that give them their service life, AC fins flatten and never recover, old mortar washes out of the wall, and lead paint predating 1978 becomes contaminated dust across your garden. On all of these the repair costs many times what the cleaning was worth.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The most expensive mistakes are the ones that look fine at the time. Water driven behind lap siding by an upward-angled stream soaks sheathing and insulation with nowhere to dry, and it surfaces a season later as damp or rot that nobody connects to an afternoon of washing. Keep the stream downward or level on anything vertical.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most of this list still needs cleaning, and the answer is soft washing: chemistry at garden hose pressure, given time to work, then rinsed gently. On organic growth it outperforms pressure anyway, because algae and mildew are killed rather than knocked off. And treat the machine itself with respect: a 1,500 PSI stream cuts skin, and that wound is an emergency rather than a scratch.
          </p>
        </div>


